  "final_text": "All four steps are done, using only the Kibana tools.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 36 fields. The time field is `timestamp`. The fields include `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `FlightDelay` (boolean), `DistanceKilometers` (number), `AvgTicketPrice` (number), `Origin`/`Dest` location fields, and `hour_of_day`/`dayOfWeek`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-bf57fc4a Flights' was created with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count by `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Flights by carrier\": a bar chart of count by `Carrier` (top 10 terms).\n\n   I chose line and bar charts because the request did not name chart types.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-bf57fc4a Cancelled flights' (`.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`) runs every 1m. It counts documents where `Cancelled` is true over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`, and fires when the count is greater than 10. The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Panel edit and read-back:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". The dashboard read-back confirms the new title. The alert rule read-back shows `enabled: true`, status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
